#include <memory>
auto p = std::make_unique<int>(42);
std::cout << *p << "\n"; // 42
// no delete needed — freed when p goes out of scope
// Custom class
struct Widget {
int x;
Widget(int v) : x(v) { std::cout << "construct\n"; }
~Widget() { std::cout << "destroy\n"; }
};
void use() {
auto w = std::make_unique<Widget>(7);
std::cout << w->x << "\n";
} // "destroy" printed here automatically
unique_ptr is move-only — there's exactly one owner at a time:
auto a = std::make_unique<int>(5);
auto b = a; // ERROR — can't copy
auto c = std::move(a); // OK — ownership transferred; a is now nullptr
Use unique_ptr for almost all heap-allocated objects. Costs nothing at runtime vs a raw pointer.

auto p1 = std::make_shared<int>(100);
auto p2 = p1; // both own; count = 2
// when both go out of scope, freed
Use when you genuinely have multiple owners — usually in observer-style or graph structures. Has reference-counting overhead, so prefer unique_ptr when one owner is enough.

auto sp = std::make_shared<int>(5);
std::weak_ptr<int> wp = sp; // doesn't extend lifetime
if (auto locked = wp.lock()) { // try to upgrade to shared_ptr
std::cout << *locked;
}
Use to break reference cycles in shared_ptr graphs, or to observe without prolonging life.
Rule: never write new or delete in new code. If you find yourself reaching for them, your design needs a smart pointer or a container.

A move transfers a resource's ownership instead of copying it.
std::vector<int> make_big() {
std::vector<int> v(1'000'000, 42);
return v; // moves (not copies) the vector out
}
auto v = make_big(); // no million-element copy
The compiler optimises returns automatically (NRVO + implicit move). You don't have to do anything for the common case.

std::string a = "hello world this is long enough to allocate";
std::vector<std::string> v;
v.push_back(std::move(a)); // moves a into v; a is now empty
// a is in a "moved-from" state — valid but unspecified value
Use std::move when:
You're passing a local variable to a sink (container, constructor) and won't use it afterward.
You're transferring ownership in an assignment or parameter.
Don't move const objects — moving requires modifying the source. The compiler silently falls back to copy.

class Person {
std::string name_;
public:
Person(std::string name) : name_(std::move(name)) {}
};
The parameter name is constructed (one copy or move depending on the caller). Then std::move transfers into name_ with zero extra allocation. Modern idiomatic style.

auto x = 5; // int
auto y = 3.14; // double
auto& r = x; // int&
const auto& cr = x; // const int&
// for long types:
auto it = v.begin(); // far better than std::vector<int>::iterator
// auto in lambdas (C++14+)
auto f = [](auto a, auto b){ return a + b; };
f(1, 2); // int
f(1.5, 2.5); // double
decltype(expr) gives you the type of an expression without evaluating it:
int x = 5;
decltype(x) y = 10; // int y = 10;
decltype(x + 0.5) z; // double z;
decltype(auto) (C++14) preserves references / cv-qualifiers in return type deduction.

// minimal
auto greet = []{ std::cout << "hi\n"; };
greet();
// with parameters
auto add = [](int a, int b){ return a + b; };
add(3, 4);
// explicit return type
auto safe_div = [](double a, double b) -> double {
if (b == 0) return 0;
return a / b;
};

constexpr int square(int n) { return n * n; }
constexpr int x = square(5); // computed at compile time → 25
int y = 10;
int z = square(y); // OK too — runs at runtime
If all arguments are compile-time constants, the call is evaluated by the compiler. Otherwise it runs at runtime.

consteval int strict_square(int n) { return n * n; }
constexpr int a = strict_square(5); // OK
int b = strict_square(y); // ERROR — y not constant

#include <optional>
std::optional<int> divide(int a, int b) {
if (b == 0) return std::nullopt;
return a / b;
}
if (auto r = divide(10, 3); r.has_value()) {
std::cout << *r; // 3
}
int safe = divide(10, 0).value_or(-1); // -1 on failure
Replaces "return -1 on failure" sentinels.

A type-safe tagged union — holds one of several types.
#include <variant>
std::variant<int, std::string, double> v;
v = 42;
v = "hello";
v = 3.14;
std::visit([](auto&& arg){
std::cout << arg << "\n";
}, v);

std::pair<int, std::string> p{1, "Ada"};
auto [id, name] = p; // id = 1, name = "Ada"
std::map<int, std::string> m{{1, "Ada"}, {2, "Bob"}};
for (const auto& [k, v] : m) {
std::cout << k << ": " << v << "\n";
}

template <typename T>
void describe(T x) {
if constexpr (std::is_integral_v<T>) {
std::cout << "integer\n";
} else if constexpr (std::is_floating_point_v<T>) {
std::cout << "float\n";
} else {
std::cout << "other\n";
}
}
Branches not taken are not even compiled. Replaces lots of old SFINAE template tricks.

Concepts let you constrain template parameters:
#include <concepts>
template <std::integral T> // T must be an integer type
T add_one(T x) { return x + 1; }
add_one(5); // OK
add_one(3.14); // ERROR — 3.14 isn't integral
Custom concept:
template <typename T>
concept Addable = requires(T a, T b) {
{ a + b } -> std::same_as<T>;
};
template <Addable T>
T sum(T a, T b) { return a + b; }
Replaces verbose enable_if patterns with readable constraints. Much friendlier compile errors.

Headers are an artefact of 1970s linker design. Modules replace them with proper compile units:
// math.cppm (module interface)
export module math;
export int square(int n) { return n * n; }
// main.cpp
import math;
int main() {
return square(5);
}
Benefits: ~10× faster compile times, no preprocessor leakage, clean dependency graph.
Tooling still maturing in 2026; most projects still use headers + #include. Worth knowing exists; adopt when your build chain is ready.

A short modern C++ example combining many features:
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
#include <optional>
class Animal {
public:
virtual ~Animal() = default;
virtual std::string speak() const = 0;
};
class Dog : public Animal {
std::string name_;
public:
Dog(std::string name) : name_(std::move(name)) {}
std::string speak() const override { return name_ + ": woof!"; }
};
class Cat : public Animal {
std::string name_;
public:
Cat(std::string name) : name_(std::move(name)) {}
std::string speak() const override { return name_ + ": meow"; }
};
std::optional<std::string> first_speaker(const std::vector<std::unique_ptr<Animal>>& zoo) {
if (zoo.empty()) return std::nullopt;
return zoo.front()->speak();
}
int main() {
std::vector<std::unique_ptr<Animal>> zoo;
zoo.push_back(std::make_unique<Dog>("Rex"));
zoo.push_back(std::make_unique<Cat>("Mochi"));
for (const auto& a : zoo) {
std::cout << a->speak() << "\n";
}
auto sorted = first_speaker(zoo);
std::cout << sorted.value_or("(empty zoo)") << "\n";
}
Features used: unique_ptr + make_unique, virtual functions, override, std::move, range-for with const auto&, optional, value_or, structured initialisation, the Rule of Zero (no explicit destructors anywhere).

